## ABSTRACT
In the past decades, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and covalent organic frameworks (COFs) basically enjoy the coordination chemistry and covalent chemistry, respectively, and such uniqueness has become the major obstacle hampering their further scope diversity and application multi-functionalization. Inspired from the principle of organic retrosynthesis, combining coordination bond and covalent bond together offers additional opportunities for constructing novel MOFs, COFs and MOF@COF hybrids as well as confer on them superior performances in versatile application fields. In this review, we firstly classify and summarize the recently reported synthesis strategies based on the integration of metal–ligand coordination and dynamic covalent bonds. Then, the application performances of as-constructed MOFs, COFs as well as MOF@COF hybrids are discussed and highlighted in the fields of adsorption, separation, catalysis, biosensing, energy storage and so on. Last, our personal insights of the remaining challenges and further prospects are also provided, in order to trigger much more inspirations and endeavors for this hot research field.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), covalent organic frameworks (COFs), hybrids, coordination chemistry, covalent chemistry, applications
## 1 Introduction
Emerging as two types of novel crystalline porous materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) [1–3] and covalent-organic frameworks (COFs) [4–6] have drawn huge attentions thanks but not limited to their ultrahigh surface area, well-ordered and uniform pore structure and easy modification and functionalization. Typically, under the guidance of reticular chemistry [7–10], MOFs are constructed via the coordination bond between metal ions/secondary metal nodes and organic ligands [11–14]; while COFs are linked via the covalent bond between different organic building blocks [15–18]. Such specific bonding type of MOFs and COFs, however, has heavily hindered their further propagation of family members as well as realization of multifunctionality by bonding design. Inspired by the retrosynthesis concept in organic chemistry, a target product is available from various synthesis routes involving specific synthons [19]. In principle, MOFs and COFs can also be disassembled into different synthon combinations via combining the chemistry of both coordination and covalent bonds. For instance, several types of unusual MOFs [20–23] have been successfully constructed via in-situ formation of covalent bonds (e.g., imine and B–O bond) using the elaborately designed metal nodes and organic ligands. Meanwhile, it is also realizable to construct COF materials [24–26]
with the aid of directional metal–ligand coordination bonds. Moreover, series of strategies for hybridization of MOFs and COFs are boomingly developed recently [27–34], which have not only expanded the scope of crystalline porous materials but also integrated both advantages of MOFs and COFs for pursing enhanced performances in versatile applications.
Over past decades, MOFs have been extensively explored in gas storage [35–37], separation [38–40], catalysis [41–49], energy conversion [50–54] and so on [55–64] by taking properties of both inorganic metal nodes and organic ligands. Nonetheless, when employed in practical applications, MOFs generally suffer from the susceptible chemical stability (e.g., inferior moisture, acid, and base stability) [65,66] caused by the relatively weak metal–ligand coordination bond. On the contrary, COFs exhibited robust chemical stability beneficial for maintaining long-term stable performance [67,68], but the limited scope of covalent bonds used for construction of COFs has seriously obstructed their type diversity and multi-functionalization. Get two birds with one stone, novel porous materials via integrating both MOF and COF chemistries have inherited both advantages and even surpassed the performance of corresponding individuals in the application of adsorption [69,70], separation [71–73], catalysis [74–79] and so on [80–85].
In this review, we firstly summarize the strategies for construction of new MOF and COF materials via simultaneously combining the chemistry of coordination and covalent bonds. Also, the emerging protocols for hybridization of MOFs and COFs have been presented. Thanks to the integrated properties, as-constructed MOFs, COFs, and MOF@COF hybrids are then highlighted and discussed in the application fields of adsorption, separation, catalysis and so on. Last but not least, our personal insights concerning on the remaining challenges and future advancing are also provided. We hope this comprehensive review will not only be helpful for rational design and synthesis of novel MOF- or/and COF-based multifunctional materials but also further expanding their application visions in the near future.
## 2 Synthetic strategies for combining MOFs and COFs
## 2.1 COFs inspired synthesis of MOFs
Due to the incompatibility between metal nodes and conventional synthesis conditions of MOFs (e.g., the indissolubility and susceptibility of metal nodes at the synthesis conditions), it is normally difficult to directly use metal nodes as starting precursors for constructing MOFs with new structures and functionalities. Fortunately, COF inspired synthesis strategies bring additional opportunities for construction of unusual MOFs via in-situ formation of dynamic \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{-}}\mathsf{N}\) and \(\scriptstyle\mathrm{B-O}\) bonds, which require prefunctionalized metal clusters as well as elaborately designed organic ligands. For example, Yaghi and co-workers [20] reported the synthesis of a new type of Ti-MOF (MOF-901) featuring unusual hexameric titanium oxo \(\left(\mathrm{Ti}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}\right)\) metal node via the dynamic imine condensation. In specification, the reaction precursors containing titanium(IV) isopropoxide \(\left(\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{OPr})_{4}\right)\), 4- aminobenzoic acid (H-AB), and benzene-1,4-dialdehyde (BDA) dissolved in methanol were solvothermally reacted in a sealed tube under inert nitrogen atmosphere at \(125\,^{\circ}\!C\) for 3 days. During the synthesis process, the in-situ formed \(\mathrm{Ti}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}(\mathrm{OPr})_{6}(\mathrm{AB})_{6}\) metal node was further linked with BDA ligand via the reversible imine condensation to construct the final MOF-901 product in good crystallinity (Fig. 1(a)). Moreover, the proposed strategy was also feasible for changing BDA to \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\) -biphenyldicarboxaldehyde
(BPDA) in the case of construction of MOF-902 under similar solvothermal treatments [86]. Noteworthily, these are the only two Ti-MOFs consisting unusual \(\mathrm{Ti}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}\) octahedron metal node.
Polyoxometalates (POMs) are a kind of classical metal clusters characteristic of structural and property diversity [87–89]. Yaghi and co-workers further extended the COF inspired synthesis strategy to build POM-based MOFs, known as MOF-688 [23]. In details, the amino functionalized Anderson type POM \(\mathrm{\small{[(N(C_{4}H_{9})_{4}]_{3}[M n M o_{6}O_{18}\{(O C H_{2})_{2}C N H_{2}\}_{2}]}}}\) (denoted as \(\mathrm{MnMo}_{6}{}^{3}\) ) was chosen as the metal node for linking the 4-connected tetrahedral tetrakis(4-formylphenyl)methylene (TFPM) ligand via imine condensation. Thanks to the introduction of highly negatively charged \(\mathrm{MnMo}_{6}{}^{3}\) cluster, MOF-688 was competent for abundant installation of lithium ions and thereby giving high ionic conductivity up to \(3.4\times10^{4}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{{-}}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) at \(20\,^{\circ}\!C.\) Recently, more POM-based MOFs have been successfully constructed via the same imine condensation between amino functionalized POM and aldehyde type building units [90–92].
In addition, Li and co-workers [22] successfully developed a catalytically active \({\mathrm{Cu(I)}}\) cyclic trinuclear unit (CTU) based MOFs by utilizing reversible imine bond. Typically, \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}},\) \(1H\cdot\) -pyrazole-4- carbaldehyde ligand (HL), and, 3,5-tris(4-aminophenyl)benzene (1) or \(^{4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}}\) -(1,3,5-triazine-2,4,6- triyl)trianiline (2) were mixed in the mesitylene/dioxane/aqueous acetic acid solution, and the resultant mixture was kept at \(120\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(72\,\mathrm{{h}}\) to obtain the JNM-1 \(\left(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{L}_{3}\mathbf{1}_{3}\right)\) or JNM-2 \(\left(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{L}_{3}\mathrm{2}_{3}\right)\) , respectively (Fig. 1(b)). Moreover, JNM-1 or JNM-2 could also be obtained by reacting the preconstructed aldehyde functionalized \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{3}{\mathrm{L}}_{3}\) with organic linker 1 or 2 via a stepwise synthesis strategy. Li's group [93] reported FDM-71 \(\mathrm{FDM}=\) Fudan materials) by via \(\mathrm{Cu-N}\) coordination. Then, the treatment of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{PyCA})_{2}\) \(\mathrm{(PyCA~=~}\) pyrazolate-4- carboxaldehyde) cluster with p-phenylenediamine (PA) under the existence of minute quantity of water at \(170\,^{\circ}\!C\) led to the final production of FDM-71. In another case, Murugavel et al. [94] successfully constructed series of novel crystalline zinc phosphate-based frameworks. Thereinto, four aldehyde groups dangling on the vertex of elaborately designed tetrahedron \((\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(\mathrm{dipp})_{4}(4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Py-}\) \(\mathrm{CHO})_{4})\) (dipp \(=\) diiminopyridine, and \(4{\cdot}\mathrm{Py}{\cdot}\mathrm{CHO}\,=\,4\) -pyridine carboxaldehyde) cluster were condensed with different types of linear diamines via imine condensation.
As glorious stars in the MOF family, zirconium-based MOFs (Zr-MOFs) have exhibited great potentials in practical applications
due to their robust stability, adjustable porosity, structural diversity, etc. [95]. When being introduced into \( \mathrm{Zr} \)-MOFs, the imine-based linkers are prone to be decomposed under the required harsh solvothermal conditions (e.g., strong acidity and high reaction temperature) [96]. Farha and co-workers [97] proposed a COFs inspired synthesis strategy for in situ formation of imine-based \( \mathrm{Zr} \)-MOFs. In the solvothermal reaction system, 4-aminobenzoic acid or \( p \)-phenylenediamine, 4-formylbenzoic acid and \( \mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2} \) were separately dissolved in N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF). Then, the mixed solution containing above two types of precursors was heated at \( 100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) overnight to yield the light-yellow NU-401 or NU-402, respectively. In which, the monocarboxylates functionalized with amino and aldehyde group not only acted as the building block for in situ formation of the imine-based linker, but also was utilized as acid modulator for improving the crystallinity of MOF products. A similar design and synthetic method was adopted by Kaskel's group [98,99], who synthesized a series of unconventional \( \mathrm{Zr} \)-MOFs containing imine-based linkers. In specification, the in situ formed dicarboxylate ligands via the imine bond (i.e., \( \mathrm{CBAB} = 4 \)-carboxybenzylidene-4-amino benzoic acid, \( \mathrm{CBHB} = 4 \)-(4-carboxyphenyl)-3-hydroxybenzoic acid, \( \mathbf{L4}^{\prime} = 4,4^{\prime} \)-(11E,1E)-(1,4-phenylenebis(azanylylidene))-bis(methanylylidene))dibenzoic acid) were further coordinated with classical \( \mathrm{Zr}_{6} \) clusters to form the PCN-161, DUT-133 \( \left[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{CBHB})_{6}\right] \) and PCN-164, respectively. In another work reported from the same group, a novel chiral diimine DUT-136 \( \left(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}(\mathrm{L})_{3}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{6}\right) \) \( \mathrm{L} = 4,4^{\prime} \)-(((1,2-diphenylethane-1,2-diyl)bis(azaneylylidene))bis(methanylylidene))-dibenzoic acid)) was successfully synthesized following the same strategy [99]. During the synthesis progress, the chiral diimine ligand was in situ formed by the condensation of 4-formylbenzoic acid with (R,R)-1,2-diphenylethylenediamine. Immediately, the formed diimine
ligand was further reacted with the \( \mathrm{Zr} \) precursor for formation of the target DUT-136 in good crystallinity, which well addressed the poor solubility of chiral diimine ligand in the reaction solution. Moreover, the hydrophobicity and steric hindrance of the introduced diphenylethylenediamine group in the chiral diimine ligand effectively reduced the risk of the competitive hydrolysis of formed imine-based linker, which ensured the production of DUT-136 in high yield.
In addition to the in-situ formation of the imine-based ligand, Zhou's group proposed to install the labile imine-based linkers via a linker-exchange method [100,101]. As a typical representation, they successfully constructed a series of non-interpenetrated isoreticular \( \mathrm{Zr} \)-MOFs via sequentially linker-exchanging [102]. First of all, UiO-67.5 was directly constructed by the classical \( \mathrm{Zr}_{6} \) cluster and azobenzene-based linker L1 (azobenzene-4,4'-dicarboxylic acid). Then, the parent UiO-67.5 was employed for production of PCN-161 via linker-exchange of L1 with L1' (4-carboxybenzylidene-4-aminobenzoic acid) of the same dimensional length. Interesting, owing to the labile nature of the imine-liker in PCN-161, liker-exchange between linkers with different lengths can also be successfully realized. For example, PCN-162–PCN-164 with a longer linker size featuring progressively increased pore dimension were obtained in sequence via dissociation of the pristine imine-based linker in PCN-161 and subsequent reinstallation of longer imine-based linker. In addition, PCN-162–PCN-164 with a long linker size tend to form interpenetrated structures; however, the corresponding non-interpenetrated MOFs were obtained. The method also applicable for further construction of the non-interpenetrated UiO-68 and UiO-69 via similar linker-exchange processes (Fig. 2(a)). It is worthy to mention that PCN-163 and PCN-164 as well as UiO-68 and UiO-69 are only reported in the interpenetrated structures via
conventional one-pot syntheses.
Besides the imine bond, B–O linkage is another type of covalent bonds and has also been widely utilized for constructing COF examples [103–105]. To achieve the goals of introducing dynamic B–O bonds into MOFs, in 2019, Feng and coworkers [21] reported a series of pore-space pacs-type CPMs (pacs = partitioned acs net, and CPMs = crystalline porous materials), which were ultimately constructed via the coordination between in situ formed acs net framework \(((\mathrm{M}1)_{1+x}(\mathrm{M}2)_{2-x}(\mathrm{OH})_{1-x}(\mathrm{O})_{x}\) \((\mathrm{L}1)_{3})\) \((\mathrm{M}1=\mathrm{In}^{3+}\), \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\); \(\mathrm{M}2=\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\); \(\mathrm{L}1=1,4\)-terephthalic acid (BDC)) and 2,4,6-tri(4-pyridinyl)-1,3,5-boroxine trimer linker deriving from the self-condensation reaction of pyridine-4-boronic acid (Fig. 2(b)). In details, M1, M2, BDC, and pyridine-4-boronic acid were dissolved in the mixture of DMF and toluene in a sealed glass vial at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) oven for 3 days to obtain the target CPM-100. The formation mechanism of pacs-type CPMs is that the metal-carboxylate trimer \(((\mathrm{M}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{1-x}(\mathrm{O})_{x}(\mathrm{COO})_{6})\) of the acs MOF-type framework is formed concurrently with the COF-type 2,4,6-tri(4-pyridinyl)-1,3,5-boroxine (tbp) trimer via selfcondensation of pyridine-4-boronic acid. Furthermore, the metal ion of \(((\mathrm{M}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{1-x}(\mathrm{O})_{x}(\mathrm{COO})_{6})\) was coordinated with the N site in the pyridine through coordination bonds to form the final pacs-type CPMs. Li's group [106] developed a one-pot method for constructing novel B-O linked \(\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{Hf})\)-MOFs, namely BUT-108 (Zr) \((\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{L}8)_{2}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{6}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2})\) \((\mathrm{L}8=4,4',4''-(1,3,5,2,4,6\)-trioxa-triborinane-2,4,6-triy)tribenzoate) and BUT-108 (Hf) \((\mathrm{Hf}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{L}8)_{2}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{6}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2})\). The cyclotrimerized boronic acid linker L8 was in situ formed by the self-condensation reaction of 4-boronobenzoic acid and was subsequently coordinated with \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\) and \(\mathrm{Hf}_{6}\) clusters, respectively.
## 2.2 Metal–ligand coordination bonds assisted synthesis of COFs
Dating back to 1983, Sauvage and co-workers introduced metal coordination bond as a template for formation of catenanes and rotaxanes [107]. In their seminal work, the predictable tetrahedral coordination geometry of \(\mathrm{Cu(I)}\) ion was exploited to arrange two
phenanthroline ligands perpendicular to each other for obtaining the catenane product. Moreover, Nitschke and co-workers [108] conceptually showed the reversible disassembly–reassembly system constructed via the directional coordination bonds. Analogously, the employment of metal–ligand coordination bond can also be helpful for synthesis of COFs, which can not only improve the crystallinity of resulting COF examples but also confer on them interesting mechanical and dynamic absorption properties. As a semi-empirical rule, the synthesis strategy of woven COF materials relied on functionalized amino or aldehyde organic ligand that was potentially available for coordination with additional metal ions. The metal ions serve as points of registry for arranging the mutually interlaced COF threads in a precisely weaving manner. Hence, the metal–ligand coordination bonds shall be stable under acid conditions normally required by the synthesis of imine-based COF materials. In 2016, Yaghi and coworkers [24] introduced metal–ligand coordination for directing syntheses of various types of crystalline woven COFs. Taking an example, the woven \(\mathrm{COF-505(Cu)}\) was synthesized in a solvothermal reaction system via linking the aldehyde functionalized \(\mathrm{Cu(I)}\)-bis\([4,4''-(1,10\)-phenanthroline-2,9-diyl)dibenzaldehyde]tetrafluoroborate \((\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PDB})_{2}(\mathrm{BF}_{4}))\) with benzidine (BZ) (Fig. 3(a)). The successful formation of \(\mathrm{COF-505(Cu)}\) with woven structure was dominated by the imine condensation between aldehyde group armed \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PDB})_{2}(\mathrm{BF}_{4})\) building block, which was pre-synthesized via a directional coordination linkage between \([\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN})_{4}]\mathrm{BF}_{4}\), \(4,4''-(1,10\)-phenanthroline-2,9-diyl)-dibenzaldehyde (PDB), and the benzidine (BZ) linker under a weak acidic condition. Noteworthily, the \(\mathrm{Cu(I)}\) not only acted as the point of registry for directing the ordered woven in situ formed threads by interlacing PDB units, but also could be reversibly removed after formation of the woven framework without destroying the overall structure. Compared with the previous COFs, the woven COFs show obvious advantages in structural elasticity and reversibility. The effective Young's moduli (neglecting the anisotropy of the elasticity) of the demetalated COF-505 and COF-505(Cu) materials were \(\sim12.5\) and \(1.3\;\mathrm{GPa}\),
respectively. Notably, this ten-fold ratio in elasticity upon demetalation of COF-505 is similar to the elasticity ratio for porous MOFs to polyethylene. Moreover, the copper(I) ions can be reversibly removed and added without loss of the COF structure. However, due to the highly interpenetrated structure that caused small internal void space, the loading capabilities of both COF-505(Cu) and COF-505 towards external guests were heavily inhibited. To resolve this issue, Yaghi's group [110] further designed the non-interpenetrated woven COF-506(Cu), which was prepared by replacing the \(\mathrm{BF_{4}^{-}}\) with the bulky diphenylphosphinate \(\left(\mathrm{PO}_{2}\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}\right)\)under solvothermal reaction conditions similar to COF-505(Cu). Surprisingly, the demetalated COF-506 exhibited a novel mode of motional dynamics, known as "adaptive inclusion", which enabled the uptake of guest molecules larger than its intrinsic pore size.
Following those, Yaghi and co-workers [111] also constructed a novel mechanical interlocking framework, COF-500(Cu), with a pts topology. In particular, the aldehyde functionalized \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PDB})_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2}\mathrm{Ph}_{2}\) as the building block was linked with the 4-connected rectangular tetrahedral \(4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime\prime\prime\prime}\) -(ethene-1,1,2,2-tetrayl)tetrakis-([1,1'-biphenyl]-4-amine) (ETTBA) ligand via imine condensation reaction to form the crystalline porous COF-500(Cu). The imine bond between the PDB unit and the ETTBA ligand made a four-membered cycle, termed as corner-sharing one-dimensional (1D) square ribbons, which were interlocked with each other to form a 3D woven framework. Interestingly, COF-500(Cu) could be also demetalized to transform into a mechanical interlocking COF-500 framework featuring a high degree of moving freedom. Moreover, immersing COF-500 into the solution of acetonitrile/chloroform containing \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN})_{4}\mathrm{BF}_{4}\) could obtain the crystalline COF-500(Cu) reversibly.
Additionally, the same group [109] reported a novel 3D noninterpenetrated woven COF-112 with a dia topology, which was formed by interweaving 1D covalent organic thread. In specification, \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) 4-(tert-butoxy carbonylamino)- aniline (NBPDA) and 2,6-pyridinedicarboxal-dehyde (PCBA)
were mixed in acetonitrile/toluene/aqueous trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) solution, and the resultant mixture was kept at \(85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(48\ \mathrm{~h~}\) . During the synthesis process, the in situ formed \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NHBoc-DIP})_{2}(\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{2}\) \(\mathrm{Boc}=t e r t\) -butyloxycarbonyl, and \(\mathrm{DIP=}\) bis(diiminopyridine)) complex was deprotected by TFA and the released amnio group was further linked with the remaining PCBA by imine condensation reaction, finally forming the COF112 product (Fig. 3(b)). It was meaningful to highlight the key role of using Boc-protected \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NHBoc-DIP})_{2}(\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{2}\) complex as the building block, which avoided the formation of polyimide precipitates before that the DIP sites were fully coordinated by cobalt ions.
Recently, Loh and co-workers [26] developed a novel strategy for the synthesis of 3D woven COFs by combining metal–ligand coordination and dynamic covalent chemistry together. First of all, two bisphenanthroline derivatives, \(4,4^{\prime}\) -(1,10-phenanthroline-2,9 diyl)dianiline (I) and 2,9-bis(4-(dimethoxymethyl)phenyl)-1,10- phenanthroline (II) were mixed with \(\mathrm{AgBF_{4}}\) in 1-butanol/1,2- dichlorobenzene/aqueous acetic acid solution, and the mixture was placed at \(120\,^{\circ}\!C\) for 3 days to obtain single-crystalline metallo- COF (mCOF-Ag). Then, the free amino groups among the mCOF-Ag could be further crosslinked with glyoxal by imine condensation reaction to construct a novel 3D woven framework, namely wCOF-Ag (Fig. 4(a)). Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) showed that there were almost no changes in crystallinity before and after the cross-linking reaction. To one's delight, the Young's modulus of crosslinked wCOF-Ag was significantly improved. The same research group further extended the synthesis strategy for construction of a 3D woven COF-Cu [112]. Detailedly, amine functionalized \(\mathrm{Cu(I)-bis[4,4^{\prime}-(1,10-}\) phenanthroline-2,9- diyl)dianiline]tetrafluoroborate \(\left(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PDB})_{2}(\mathrm{BF}_{4})\right)\) and acetal functionalized \(\mathrm{Cu(I)-bis[2,9-bis(4-(dimethoxymethyl)phenyl)-1,10-}\) phenanthroline]tetrafluoroborate \(\left(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{PDB-OCH}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{BF}_{4})\right)\) building blocks were reacted under solvothermal conditions to obtain the 3- fold interpenetrated wCOF-Cu with a dia topology (Fig. 4(b)). Specifically, the 1D metallo-COFs-Cu (mCOFs-Cu) was obtained by linker exchange of PDB with 2,9-bis(4-
(dimethoxymethyl)phenyl)-1,10-phenanthroline (PDB-OCH\(_3\)) under solvothermal conditions similar to synthesis of wCOF-Cu. Interestingly, the hydrogen bonding between amine groups and \(\mathrm{BF_{4}^{-}}\) anions and the \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking of adjacent phenanthroline rings both contributed greatly for increasing the mechanical strength of the resultant woven framework.
## 2.3 Synthesis of MOF and COF hybrids
Currently, numerous studies have shown that MOF-based hybrid materials could afford multifunctional properties via hybridization with other functional materials (e.g., metal nanoparticles [113–116], polymers [117, 118], and other MOFs [119, 120]. Similarly, combining functional components with COFs to obtain COF-based hybrid materials has also attracted widespread concerns [121–126]. Recently, researchers have successfully achieved the hybridization of MOFs and COFs, which can be further divided into two categories: MOF@COF hybrid materials synthesized by introducing the prepared MOFs into the synthetic system of COFs and COF@MOF hybrid materials prepared via the growth of additional MOFs outside/inside the pre-synthesized COFs. Accordingly, the additional covalent bonding and metal–ligand coordination linking the two components together shall be also compatible with the synthesis conditions of hybrids. In 2018, Zhang's group [27] proposed a novel \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-68@TPACOF core–shell material by hybridizing MOFs with COFs (Fig. 5(a)). Specifically, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-68 [127] was synthesized and further modified with tris(4-formylphenyl)amine (TFPA), one of the structural units of TPA-TTPA-COF [128], to prepare the TFPA modified aldehyde-functionalized \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-68(CHO). After charging of tris(4-aminophenyl) amine (TAPA), target TPA-COF
was grafted on \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-68 homogeneously via imine condensation reaction. Following that, MOF@COF hybrid materials boomingly emerged. For instances, Qu's group [129] reported a biomimetic MOF@COF hybrid nanozyme. At first, \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and 2-aminoterephthalic acid \(\scriptstyle(2-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -BDC) were dissolved in DMF, and the resulting mixture was heated at \(110\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) to produce \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-88B(Fe). Then, the surface of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - MIL-88B(Fe) was modified with 3,5-triformylphloroglucinol (Tp). Finally, \(4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}\) -(1,3,5-triazine-2,4,6-trid)trianiline (TTA) was added for processing the condensation reaction to prepare the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-88B(Fe)@COF\(_{\mathrm{TP-TTA}}\) hybrid material. Samori and coworkers [130] fabricated an aza-MOF@COF hybrid porous material by the post-synthetic modification of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66@COF-LZU1 via the aza-Diels-Alder cycloaddition reaction [131, 132]. Particularly, the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66 was prepared by reacting \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) and \(2{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -BDC in DMF at \(120\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) . Next, the aldehyde armed \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66(CHO) was obtained after modification of 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxaldehyde (TFB), and was further reacted with \(p\) - phenylenediamine (PDA) to form a COF-LZU1 layer on the surface of parent \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66. Finally, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66@COF-LZU1, phenylacetylene, boron trifluoride diethyl etherate \((\mathrm{BF}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OEt}_{2})\) , and chloranil were added into toluene, and the mixture was heated under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) at \(110\,^{\circ}\!C\) for 1–3 days to obtain the aza-MOF@COF hybrid structure.
Besides, Kim group reported [133] an interfacial control strategy for concise and convenient construction of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti)@LZU1 hybrid material free of tedious functionalization steps. Briefly, the pre-synthesized \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti) \(\mathrm{(Ti_{8}O_{8}(O H)_{4}(N H_{2}{\cdot}B D C)_{6})}\) was mixed with TFB and PDA in 1,4- dioxane solution containing acetic acid, and the resulting mixture
was sealed for 3 days at room temperature to produce the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125(Ti)@LZU1 (Fig. 5(b)). Similarly, Li's group [134] developed a stable core-shell \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@NTU hybrid material by a simple one-step method. That was, 4-formylphenylboronic acid (4-FPBA) and 1,3,5-tris(4-aminophenyl)-benzene (TAPB) as two kinds of building units of NTU-COF were mixed with the pre-synthesized \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) \((\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{BDC})_{3})\) suspension to obtain \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@FPBA-TAPB-COF. By adjusting the added ratio of TAPB to 4-FPBA, the formation of imine bonds and boroxine rings can be varied, allowing NTU-COF-1 to be controllably wrapped on the surface of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe). More hybridizations of MOFs and COFs have been reported via the similar one-pot strategy [69, 70, 85, 135].
In addition, Zhao's group [136] constructed the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66@TpPa-1 hybrid material by utilizing an amorphous polymer intermediated method. As the first step, Tp and p-phenylenediamine (Pa) were added to the pre-synthesized \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 \((\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{BDC})_{12})\) suspension. As a result, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 nanoparticles encased with an amorphous polyimine layer were obtained after the reaction at \(80~^{\circ}\!C\) for \(3\mathrm{~h~}\). As the second step, the resultant MOF@polyimine was dispersed in a mesitylene/1,4-dioxane mixture, which was subsequently incubated at \(120\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 3 days in order to accomplish the transformation of amorphous polyimine to crystalline TpPa-1. Moreover, Maspoch's group [137] synthesized a type of microspherical \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66@COF-TAPB-BTCA composite bead by a similar strategy. Firstly, the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 nanocrystals were successfully encapsulated into amorphous imine-based TAPB-BTCA (TAPB = 1,3,5-tris-(4-aminophenyl)benzene, \(\mathrm{BTCA} = 1,3,5\)-benzenetricarbaldehyde) polymer beads through spray drying. Secondly, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66@polymer beads were transformed to target \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66@COF-TAPB-BTCA under dynamic covalent chemistry conditions. That was, the beads were dispersed into a mixture of 1,4-dioxane and mesitylene (9:1 volume/volume), and then water and acetic acid were added to the resulting dispersion, which was heated at \(80~^{\circ}\!C\) for \(72\,\mathrm{{h}}\) to obtain the crystalline \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66@COF-TAPB-BTCA. Moreover, Zhao's group [29] prepared the \(\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66@COF-1 hybrid material in a sandwich fashion (Fig. 5(c)). First, \(\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 prepared by the typical wet impregnation strategy was mixed with 1,4-phthalaldehyde to obtain the aldehyde-functionalized \(\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66(CHO). Subsequently, \(\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66(CHO) was connected with TTA to form amorphous \(\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66@Polymer. Then, by replacing 1,4-phthalaldehyde with 2,5-dihydroxytereph-thalaldehyde linker, the \(\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{UiO}\)-66-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)@polymer underwent crystallization transformation with the assistance of acetic acid at \(120\,^{\circ}\!C\) for 3, 4, 5, and 6 days to obtain \(\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66@COF-n (COF-n = COF-3d, COF-4d, COF-5d, COF-6d, respectively) hybrid material. More successful cases including \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125(Ti)@DM-LZU1 [138] (DM-LZU1 = dinitohylded COF-LZU1), and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125(Ti)@Pt@DM-LZU1 with sandwich structure have been offered via utilizing a similar strategy.
Not merely growing COFs onto the surface of MOFs, alternatively, the study of \(\mathrm{COF@MOF}\) hybrids has been proposed in recent years [30, 69, 139, 140]. For example, Lan's group [28] utilized a one-step strategy to achieve MOF growth onto the surface of COF by covalent bonds, namely \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66@TpPa-1-COF \((\mathrm{Tp} = 1,3,5\)-triformylphloroglucinol, \(\mathrm{Pa} = \mathrm{p}\)-phenylenediamine). Besides, Xu and co-workers [141] prepared \(\mathrm{TpPa@UiO-66-(COOH)_{2}}\) hybrid materials with yolk–shell-structured via a template-free solvothermal method. Importantly, a hollow cavity between the \(\mathrm{UiO-66-(COOH)_{2}}\) core and the TpPa-COF shell was formed during the transformation of the polyimide shell from amorphous to crystalline COF shell.
Fu and co-workers [71] reported the graft of \(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})\) (DABCO = 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane) and ZIF-8 \((\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{12}\mathrm{N}_{4}\mathrm{Zn})\) onto the COF-300 membrane. First, the aqueous solution of acetic acid was added to 1,4-dioxane solution dissolving terephthalaldehyde and tetra-(4-anilyl)-methane, and the resultant COF-300 mother solution was heated at \(100\,^{\circ}\!C\) for 3 days in an autoclave to form COF-300 membrane on the polyaniline (PANI)-modified porous \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) disk. As-formed COF-300 membrane was immersed into the \(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})\) mother solution, which was prepared by mixing \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), BDC, DABCO in DMF. Finally, the mixture was heated at \(120\,^{\circ}\!C\) for 2 days to obtain [COF-300]-\((\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})\)] composite membrane via the hydrogen bond interaction between BDC linker and the dangling \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) of COF-300. Similarly, ZIF-8 was also successfully coated onto the surface of COF-300 through the formation of coordination bond between \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) and \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) group to obtain [COF-300]-[ZIF-8] composite membrane.
In addition, Caro group [142] recently presented the example of MOF-in-COF concept, i.e., growth of ultrasmall MOF unit within pores of COF through a two-step immersion method (Fig. 6(a)). As the first step, the prepared TpPa-1 COF layers were fully immersed into the \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) methanolic solution for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\), so that \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) was adsorbed on the pore wall of TpPa-1 through the coordination interaction between \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and the imine \(\mathrm{N}\) sites among TpPa-1. Next, after adding 2-methylimidazole (2-meIM), ultrasmall ZIF-67 unit was rapidly formed within the 1D pores of TpPa-1. As confirmed by energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDXS) mapping, successive TpPa-1 layers of approximately \(1\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) thickness were deposited on the surface of \(\mathrm{d-Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) substrate, and the cobalt signals from ZIF-67 were well distributed in the composite film. The characteristic X-ray diffraction peaks of TpPa-1 have been clearly observed from the XRD pattern of ZIF-67-in-TpPa-1 membrane, whereas corresponding characteristic peak of ZIF-67 has not been discerned due to its ultrasmall dimension.
## 3 Versatile applications
The distinctive features of those unusual MOFs, COFs and MOF@COF hybrids stemming from combination of the metal coordination chemistry of MOFs and dynamic covalent chemistry of COFs have endowed them with multifunctionalities for exerting superior performances in adsorption/separation, catalysis, detections, etc.
## 3.1 Adsorption and separation
## 3.1.1 Adsorption
The accessible internal space of porous frameworks confers them great power in molecule adsorptions. Compared with the previous COFs, the woven COF-506(Cu) show obvious advantages in structural elasticity and reversibility, and even the adaptive inclusion ability towards guest molecules. Yaghi's group [110] tested the molecule adsorption capability of woven COF-506(Cu) and COF-505(Cu). Via using tetrahydrofuran (THF) as a probe molecule, COF-506(Cu) exhibited a maximum uptake of 36.6 \(\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at the relative pressure \((P/P_{0})\) of 0.98, while the THF uptake of COF-505(Cu) was ignorable at the same vapor pressure caused by its inaccessible interpenetrated network. What's more, methyl orange (MO), hydroxynaphthol blue disodium salt (HN) and methyl blue (MB) with increasing molecular size were selected for further testing. Also, COF-506(Cu) showed remarkable adsorption capacities towards MO and HN (Figs. 7(a) and 7(b)), but very small capacity towards MB due to its larger dimensionality than the intrinsic pore size of COF-506(Cu) (Fig. 7(c)). To one's surprise, the adsorption uptake towards MB of the demataled COF-506 was 11.6-fold higher than that of pristine COF-506(Cu) (Fig. 7(d)). This virtue of COF-506 after demetallation could considerably improve the flexibility and mobility for adaptation and accommodation of MB. Despite lots
of progresses achieved, the industrial application of MOFs in the adsorption field is seriously restricted, caused by their poor moisture stability [145, 146]. Currently, numerous of protocols have emerged in order to arm MOFs with good water resistance [147–150]. For instance, Farha and co-workers [97] reported the introduction of hydrophobic phenylacetylene into NU-401 via the addition reaction of imine bonds. As-obtained NU-401-Q remained an oxygen uptake up to \(8.8\;\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) comparable to that of HKUST-1 [151], but more significantly showed much improved water resistance for maintaining long-term performance ability. As a proof of concept, more than \(75\%\) of the surface area of NU-401-Q was retained while NU-401 completely lost its porosity, after immersing NU-401 and NU-401-Q in deionized water for \(20\,\mathrm{{h}}\).
Additionally, a series of MOF@COF hybrid materials emerged and exhibited dramatically improved adsorption properties than isolated MOFs or COFs thanks to the synergistic effect between two components [152–154]. Maspoch and coworkers [137] reported that the microspherical \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66@COF-TAPB-BTCA hybrid exhibited excellent performance in both \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) adsorption uptake. It was reasoned that an amount of micropores and mesopores were generated at the interface of resultant hybrid material. As verified by the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption measurement at 77 K, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66@COF-TAPB-BTCA showed a surface area of \(1,153\;\mathrm{~m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), approximately 3-fold higher of \(420\;\mathrm{~m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) of the physical mixture of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66 and COFTAPB-BTCA. Moreover, under the same \(P/P_{0}\) point, the hybrid showed a water sorption value of \(0.26\;\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), more than three times of the physical mixture \((0.07\;\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1})\). In addition, Feng's group [21] developed a pore-partitioned materials tpb-pacs CPMs with many exposed Lewis's acid sites by integrating both COF and MOF merits. The gas adsorption studies demonstrated the higher adsorption capacity of tpb-pacs CPMs towards common gasses compared with the nonpartitioned acs-type materials [155, 156].
Among them, the absorption performance towards \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) was impressively high \((0.598~\mathrm{~g}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-3})\), close to the world-record holders of MFM-300 (Al) \(\mathrm{[Al_{2}(OH)_{2}(BPTC)(NH_{3})_{6.5}}\), \(\mathrm{BPTC} = 3,3',5,5'\)-biphenyl-tetracarboxylic acid] \((0.622~\mathrm{~g}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-3})\) [157] and \(\mathrm{Co_{2}Cl_{2}BBT A}\) (BBTA = 1H,5H-benzo(1,2-d),(4,5-d)bistriazole) \((0.610\,\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-3})\) [158] at that time.
## 3.1.2 Separation
COFs are considered as potential materials for gas separation thanks but not limited to their high porosity, large surface area, and excellent thermochemical stability [159]. However, the too large pore size of conventional COFs is disadvantageous for achieving high selectivity, especially for sieving gas molecules of small dimensional differentiation. Fortunately, hybridizations of MOFs and COFs provide additional opportunities for pursing enhanced selectivity in applications of gas separation. As a typical example, Qiu and co-workers [71] fabricated \(\mathrm{COF@MOF}\) composite membranes for the selective separation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) gas mixture (Fig 8(a)). The permeability of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) gas surpasses that of the other gases \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) through the COF-300 membrane, \(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})\) membrane, and [COF-300]- \(\left[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})\right]\) composite membrane (Fig. 8(b)). Impressively, the separation factors (SFs) toward the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (1:1) binary mixture of resulting \(\left[\mathrm{COF-300}\right]-\left[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})\right]\) and \(\left[\mathrm{COF-300}\right]-\left[\mathrm{ZIF-8}\right]\) composite membranes were 12.6 and 13.5, respectively, far higher than those of the individual COF-300
(6.0), \(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})\) (7.0) and ZIF-8 (9.1) membranes. The formed amorphous MOF interlayer was considered as the key point for acquiring superior performance, which not only inherited a high gas permeability from the parent crystalline ZIF8, but also tightly filled the cracks among COF grains for improving separation selectivity. Similarly, Ben reported a [COF-300]-[UiO-66] composite membrane [160] for separation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) mixture. According to the testup results, the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) selectivity followed the sequence of [COF-300]-[UiO-66] (17.2) > UiO-66 (9.2) > COF-300 (6.0). Moreover, the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) permeability of the [COF-300]-[UiO-66] composite membrane (118,962.3 Barrer) was also higher than that of the individual UiO-66 (70,143.6 Barrer) and COF-300 (109,020.3 Barrer) membranes. The classical dissolution–diffusion mechanism can explain the superior separation performance of the composite membrane [161]. Namely, the pore sizes among composite membrane (COF-300 and UiO-66 were 2 and \(1\ \mathrm{nm}\), respectively) larger than the gas molecules sizes \(\mathrm{H}_{2}=0.289\ \mathrm{nm}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}=0.33\ \mathrm{nm}\) ) ensured the rapid pervasion of these gas molecules. Besides, the much lower isosteric adsorption enthalpies (Qst) towards \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) (COF-300 ≈3–7 \(\mathrm{kJ}\cdot\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) and \(\mathrm{UiO-66}\,\approx\,6{-}8\,\mathrm{\kJ{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}\) ) compared to that towards \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) \(\left(\mathrm{COF-300}\;\approx\;32{-}33\;\;\mathrm{kJ{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}\right)\) and \(\mathrm{UiO-66}\;\approx\;25\;\;\mathrm{kJ{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}\) ) favored the high \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) selectivity.
Moreover, Caro and co-workers [142] fabricated novel MOF-inCOF membrane for gas separation via restricting the growth of ZIF-67 unit inside pores of TpPa-1 layers. Gas-separation
performance was measured by placing the MOF-in-COF membrane into a home-made module with \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) as sweep gas (Fig. 8(c)). As-resultant ZIF-67-in-TpPa-1 membrane showed superior permeability toward \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) (3,800 GPU) than those of the other gases \(\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2},\mathrm{CH}_{4},\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6},\right.\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}\) was about 97, 99, 31, 18, respectively) (Fig. 8(d)). In addition, the real mixed-gas SFs of the ZIF-67-in-TpPa-1 membrane for equimolar \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2},\,\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4},\,\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6},\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}\) gas pairs could reach 34.9, 33.3, 110.5, and 192.7, respectively, much higher than that of pure TpPa-1-based membrane. To prove the universality of proposed concept, other MOF-in-COF types of membranes like ZIF-8-in-TpPa-1 and ZIF-67-in-TpBD were also prepared and showed superior \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) separation performance than isolated MOF- or/and COF-based membranes.
Alternatively, adding MOF@COF fillers into traditional polymer matrix to form mixed matrix membranes (MMMs) can effectively improve the gas separation performance than pristine polymers. Zhao's group [136] reported that UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) TpPa1 based MMMs showed enhanced \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivity than commercial polysulfone (PSf). It was suggested that the TpPa-1 shell featured with large pore \((18.3\mathrm{~\AA~})\) constructed fast gas permeation channels in the resultant MMMs. Moreover, both the good compatibility and dispersibility of TpPa1 shell were feasibly realized owing to their similar organic compositions to the PSf matrix, which were favorable for acquiring high separation selectivity. As expected, the size-sieving MOF pores endowed UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) TpPa-1/PSf MMMs with a high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivity of 46.7, which had an increment of \(79\%\) than the 26.1 of pure PSf membrane. The UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) TpPa1/PSf MMMs also maintained excellent \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivity after a successive \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) separation testing of \(100\ \mathrm{h},\) which was attributed to the high chemical stability of added UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) TpPa-1 fillers as well as their strong interfacial adhesion to the PSf matrix.
## 3.2 Catalysis
## 3.2.1 Photocatalysis
As a class of porous crystalline materials with molecular tunability and conjugated skeletons, COFs are a type of promising candidate for photocatalysis applications [162, 163]. However, their photocatalytic activity is hampered due to their rare active sites
and inherent high exciton binding energy [164, 165]. MOFs, by virtue of the abundance of active site and semiconductor-like band structure, are considered as another nominator but suffer from spontaneous and fast exciton quench [166–172]. By combining dynamic covalent chemistry of COFs with coordination chemistry of MOFs, novel MOFs, COFs as well as MOF@COF hybrids have been developed in the applications of photodegradation of pollutants, hydrogen generation, and organic transformation reactions [28, 76, 77, 133, 173].
As a pioneering work, Zhang's group reported [27] that \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - MIL-68@TPA-COF core–shell hybrid materials for photocatalytic degradation of rhodamine B (Rh B). Under visible light irradiation \((\lambda>420\ \mathrm{nm})\) , the ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) absorption spectra demonstrated the intensity of the typical absorption peak of Rh B at around \(554~\mathrm{nm}\) decreased with time in the existence of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - MIL-68@TPA-COF (Fig. 9(a)). Moreover, the hybrid material had a higher degradation rate constant \((0.077\ \mathrm{min^{-1}})\) than pure \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - MIL-68 \((0.054\,\mathrm{\min^{-1}})\) (Fig. 9(b)). According to the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption isotherms and Kubelka-Munk function results, the superior catalytic degradation performance of the hybrid materials was mainly resulted from the increased surface area \((539\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) as well as the lowered band gap \((2.21\ \mathrm{eV})\) of the hybrid materials. In addition, the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti) \(@\) TTB-TTA hybrid material with covalent heterojunction showing enhanced photocatalytic ability for the decomposition of the methyl orange (MO) and colorless phenol was reported by Cai' s group [174]. Using MO as the example, the rate constant of MO photodegradation followed the sequence of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti) \(@\) TTB-TTA \((0.20\,\mathrm{\min^{-1}})\,>\,\mathrm{TTB}.\) - TTA \((0.086\;\mathrm{min^{-1}})>{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti) \((0.021\;\mathrm{min^{-1}})\) . It suggested that the efficient charge transfer from the conduction band of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - MIL-125(Ti) to the valence band of TTB-TTA was determinant for the superior performance. That was, electrons in the conduction band of TTB-TTA were captured by \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to form \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) for oxidation of MO while holes remaining in the valence band of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti) could react with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) to generate OH- for further degradation of MO. Moreover, thanks to the robust stability of TTB-TTA COF serving as a protection shell, NH2-MIL125(Ti) \(@\) TTB-TTA maintained the photocatalytic conversion no less than \(85.1\%\) after 4 successive photocatalytic tests. Besides, Peng and co-workers [175] studied the photocatalytic activity of IR-MOF3@COF-LZU1 hybrid material for the degradation of p-tetrophenol (PNP). Under the irradiation of visible light, IR
MOF3@COF-LZU completely degraded the yellow PNP after only \(3.5\ \mathrm{h}\), which surpassed the degradation rate of most conventional photocatalysts [176, 177].
Photocatalytic water splitting using solar energy is a very promising way to produce clean hydrogen energy [178–180] and photoactive materials based on MOF@COF hybrid materials have recently emerged as efficient photocatalysts. Lan and coworkers [28] grafted \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66 onto TpPa-1-COFs via the covalent bonds and utilized the resultant \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66@TpPa-1-COF hybrid material for photocatalytic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) evolution. After composition optimization, the hydrogen evolution rate reached its maximum \((23.41\;\;\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1})\) via using \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66@TpPa-1-COF (4:6, molar ratio) as the photocatalyst, which was nearly 20 times higher than the sole TpPa-1-COF \((1.22\;\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1})\) . The catalytic mechanism based on the DFT calculation and TEM results indicated that the photogenerated electrons were rapidly transferred from the valence band of TpPa-1-COF to the conduction band of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66 with the assistance of conjugated \(-C{=}\mathrm{N}-\) bonds. Consequently, the separated electrons in \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66 efficiently reduced \(\mathrm{H^{+}}\) to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) with the assistance of Pt co-catalyst, and holes remaining at the TpPa-1-COF were caught by sodium ascorbate for accomplishing the catalytic cycle smoothly. Also, Zou's group [144] prepared a benzoic acidmodified covalent triazine-based framework (B-CTF-1), which was further integrated with \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti) via covalent amide bonds in order to purse improved \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production rate. Thanks to the amide covalent bonds playing a charge-mediating role for promoted charger transfer process, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti)/B-CTF-1 hybrid exerted a \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) evolution rate up to \(360~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) under visible light irradiation, which was over two-fold higher of that of the sole CTF-1. Moreover, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti)/B-CTF-1 maintained good catalytic activity after four recycle photocatalysis.
Besides, Jiang's group [181] constructed \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66@TFPTDETH \(\mathrm{TFT}=\) 1,3,5-tris-(4-formyl-phenyl) triazine, and DETH \(=~2{,}5\) -diethoxybenzene-1,4-dicarbohydrazide) hetero-framework for photocatalytic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) evolution. By varying the amount of TFP \(^{\mathrm{~\,~}}\) (0.013, 0.026, 0.052 and \(0.078\ \mathrm{mmol})\) and DETH (0.019, 0.038, 0.076, and \(0.114\ \mathrm{mmol})\) , a series of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66@TFPT-DETH samples were prepared and denoted as \(\mathrm{U}@\mathrm{TDE}n\) \((n=1,\,2,\,4\) and
Besides, Jiang's group [181] constructed \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66@TFPT-DETH \(\mathrm{TFT}=\) 1,3,5-tris-(4-formyl-phenyl) triazine, and DETH \(=~2{,}5\) -diethoxybenzene-1,4-dicarbohydrazide) hetero-framework for photocatalytic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) evolution. By varying the amount of TFP \(^{\mathrm{~\,~}}\) (0.013, 0.026, 0.052 and \(0.078\ \mathrm{mmol})\) and DETH (0.019, 0.038, 0.076, and \(0.114\ \mathrm{mmol})\) , a series of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66@TFPT-DETH samples were prepared and denoted as \(\mathrm{U}@\mathrm{TDE}n\) \((n=1,\,2,\,4\) and 6), respectively. Thanks to the broadened visible light absorption and boosted charge transfer rate of the hetero-framework, \(\mathrm{U}@\mathrm{TDE}\) presented low interfacial charge transfer resistance and large photocurrent density. Among them, \(\mathrm{U}@\mathrm{TDE}_{4}\) with an optimized thickness of \(20.6~\mathrm{nm}\) exerted the maximum hydrogen evolution rate \((7178\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1})\) , while pristine TFP \(^{\mathrm{~\,~}}\) -DETH was only \(2501\;\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) (Fig 9(c)). Moreover, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66@TFPTDPP \(\mathrm{U}@\mathrm{TDP4})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66@TFPT-DMTH \((\mathrm{U}@\mathrm{TDM4})\) were also synthesized via varying the side chains among COF shell from the original TFP \(^{\mathrm{~\,~}}\) -DETH to TFP \(^{\mathrm{~\,~}}\) -DETH-methoxy, TFPTDPP-propoxy \(\mathrm{DPP}=2{,}5\) -dipropoxy-1,4-dihydrazide) and TFPTDMTH-ethoxy (2,5-dimethoxyterephthalohydrazide). The \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) evolution rate ranked as the sequence of \(\mathrm{U}@\mathrm{TDE}_{4}>\mathrm{U}@\mathrm{TDP}_{4}>\) \(\mathrm{U}@\mathrm{TDM}_{4}\) (Fig. 9(d)), owing to the decreased interlayer charge transfer efficiencies arising from the side chain weakened \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking and aggregation [182, 183].
Photo-triggered organic transformation via using MOF and COF hybrids is also an intriguing research topic due to the improved photocatalytic activity and product selectivity originating from the synergistic effect. For example, Kim and coworkers [133] reported \(\mathrm{Pd/NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti)@LZU1 hybrid materials as photocatalysts for olefin hydrogenation under visible light. Among "donor–mediator–acceptor" multifunctional systems, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti) core acts as an electron donor; LZU1 shell functions as an intermediary for electron migration, and Pd accepts and utilizes electrons for catalytic hydrogenation. Consequently, \(\mathrm{Pd/NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti)@LZU1 nearly completely converted styrene to ethylbenzene \((>\,99\%)\) under visible light irradiation of \(15\ \mathrm{min}\) . In contrast, Pd/LZU1 and \(\mathrm{Pd/NH}_{2}\) -MIL125(Ti) gave a low conversion of \(42\%\) and \(83\%\) , respectively, under other identical reaction conditions. In addition, ammonia borane (AB) with high hydrogen content could efficiently produce hydrogen through using \(\mathrm{Pd/NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti)@LZU1 as the photocatalyst. Bearing these in mind, a dual-chamber microreactor was designed for the coupled photocatalytic dehydrogenation of AB and the hydrogenation of olefins. In which, a styrene conversion rate up to \(94\%\) after reaction time of \(68\ \mathrm{s}\) and \(23\%\) conversion rate of AB after \(20{\cdot}\mathrm{min}\) reaction were achieved.
Photo-triggered organic transformation via combining chemistry of MOFs and COFs is also an intriguing research topic due to the improved photocatalytic activity and product selectivity originating from the synergistic effect. For example, MOF-901 [20] composed of unusual hexameric titanium oxo \(\left(\mathrm{Ti}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}\right)\) metal node and benzene-1,4-dialdehyde linker, demonstrates high performance in photocatalytic polymerization of methyl methacrylate (MMA) under visible-light irradiation. MOF-901 afforded polyMMA in the yield of \(87\%\), molecular weight (Mn) of \(26,850\ \mathrm{g\cdot mol^{-1}}\) and corresponding polydispersity index (PDI) of 1.6, which is much superior to the commercially available Degussa \(\mathrm{P-25\,TiO_{2}}\) (yield \(=52\%\), \(M_{\mathrm{n}}=17,000\ \mathrm{g\cdot mol^{-1}}\), \(\mathrm{PDI}=1.6\)), and \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) (13%, \(18,500\ \mathrm{g\cdot mol^{-1}}\), 1.6). What's more, MOF-902 [86] was composed of a broader conjugation skeleton and therefore possessed a lower band gap energy \((2.50\ \mathrm{eV})\) than MOF-901 (2.65 eV), which was favorable for harvesting light energy. When using MOF-902 as the photocatalyst, the produced PMMA had characteristics of a higher \(M_{\mathrm{n}}\) \((31,465\ \mathrm{g\cdot mol^{-1}})\), higher yield \((84\%)\), and much lower PDI (1.11). Moreover, MOF-902 was applicable for photo-catalyzing the self-polymerization of benzyl methacrylate (BMA) and styrene (St) monomers. The produced polyBMA presented a \(M_{\mathrm{n}}\) of \(32,050\ \mathrm{g\cdot mol^{-1}}\) and PDI of 1.11, while the produced polySt gave a Mn of \(6660\ \mathrm{g\cdot mol^{-1}}\) and PDI of 1.65.
## 3.2.2 Thermocatalysis
Knoevenagel condensation reaction is widely used in the synthesis of coumarin, cosmetic and pharmaceutical intermediates, which can be satisfactorily catalyzed by materials containing basic sites (e.g., \(-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}-\)) and accessible pore structures.
Murugavel's group [94] reported a novel imine-linked polycrystalline zinc phosphate framework for Knoevenagel reactions between 4-nitrobenzaldehyde and malononitrile. The reactants could be completely converted at \(60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) after reaction of \(20\ \mathrm{h}\), thanks to rich basic \(-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}-\) sites and accessible pores of the constructed framework. In addition, by taking advantage of lability of the dynamic imine bond, introduction of the diamine fragment
into PCN-164 framework was achieved by Zhou's group for further tuning the basicity [102]. For instance, the phenylenediamine fragments of PCN-164 frameworks were successfully exchanged to cyclohexane-1,4-diamine (CHDA) or 2,5-dimethylbenzene-1,4-diamine (DBDA) by utilizing reversible dissociation/reassociation of imine bonds (Fig. 10(a)). Using CHDA-exchanged PCN-164 for demonstration, its catalytic conversion for Knoevenagel reactions between 4-nitrobenzaldehyde and malononitrile was improved to \(99\%\), higher than the \(58\%\) of pristine PCN-164.
As one of the widely used raw materials for the Knoevenagel condensation reaction, aromatic aldehydes are prone to be oxidized in priority under aerobic environment. To solve this problem, Gao and co-workers [135] designed a PCN-222-Co@TpPa-1 hybrid material featuring both Lewis acid and Bronsted base active sites for the one-pot cascade deacetalization-Knoevenagel condensation reaction. In details, (dimethoxymethyl)-benzene was first transformed into benzaldehyde by the Lewis acid \(\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{IV})\) clusters and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\) centers exposed by the PCN-222-Co core. Subsequently, the \(-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}-\) dangling on TpPa-1 shell acted as Bronsted base site to catalyze the conversion of benzaldehyde intermediate and malononitrile to 2-benzylidenemalononitrile product. Impressively, PCN-222-Co@TpPa-1 showed a high conversion up to \(99.3\%\) at \(50\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) after \(10\ \mathrm{h}\) as well as outstanding recyclability.
It has been previously reported that the surface and channel of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) are both hydrophilic [184]. So that for hydrophobic substrates like styrene, the application of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) in its catalytic conversion is seriously limited. Li and coworkers [134] coated a hydrophobic NTU-COF shell on \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe) to obtain \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@NTU hybrid material by a one-step method. In the oxidation of styrene, the as-designed \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Fe)@NTU hybrid showed higher conversion (32%) and selectivity (84%) than sole \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101 (24% conversion and 27% selectivity) and NTU COF (10% conversion and 67% selectivity) contributed by the synergistic effect between \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-
101(Fe) and NTU-COF. Namely, NTU-COF shell promoted the mass transfer of hydrophobic styrene, while \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101 (Fe) provided coordinated unsaturated catalytic sites for efficiently conversion of incoming styrene. The hybridization of MOFs and COFs not only inherits their original features but also presents synergistic effect beyond the individual component to improve the performance for various functional applications. For example, Zhao's group [29] designed a sandwiched catalytic Pd/UiO-66-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) COF-1 platform for hydrogenation of olefins including cyclohexene, trans-stilbene, and triphenylethylene (Fig. 10(b)). Pd/UiO-66-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) COF-3d with COF-1 shell thickness of \(30\ \mathrm{nm}\) and pore size of \(2.5\ \mathrm{nm}\) was used as a catalytic testing example. As the molecular sizes of cyclohexene \((5.39\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times5.02\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) and trans-stilbene \((11.65\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times5.06\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) were significantly smaller than that of the pore size of COF-1, the Pd/UiO-66-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) COF-3d composite exhibited a yield of \(98.8\%\) for cyclohexene and \(87.1\%\) for trans-stilbene. Distinctively, triphenylethylene \((10.71\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times9.2\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times9.1\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) that was difficult to be diffused through the COF-1 shell of Pd/UiO-66-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) COF-3d was negligible converted (Fig. 10(c)). The synergy between the three type components among sandwiched Pd/UiO-66-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) COF-1 endowed them with high size-selective catalytic performance: Namely, Pd nanoparticles served as catalytically active sites; \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) core acted as porous support, and COF-1 shell played a size-selective regulator role.
## 3.2.3 Electrocatalysis
Wang's group [82] prepared highly dispersed \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) onto N-doped porous carbon (NPC) via the calcination of ZIF-67@BFC hybrid materials \((\mathrm{ZIF-67}=\) zeolitic imidazolate frameworks, BFC \(=\) benzoic acid modified covalent organic framework). By increasing the BFC content from \(0.25\) to \(1\ \mathrm{g}\), \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC-1}\), \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC-2}\), \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC-3}\), \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC-4}\) samples were obtained respectively, and then tested as the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) catalysts. Among them, the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC-2}\) exerted a smaller Tafel slope \((79\ \mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{decade}^{-1})\) and higher mass activity of \(130\ \mathrm{A}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at the overpotential of \(400\ \mathrm{mV}\), compared to those of other \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPCs}\) \((92\ \mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{decade}^{-1})\) and \(5.3\ \mathrm{A}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC-1}\), \(101\ \mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{decade}^{-1}\) and \(1.9\ \mathrm{A}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC-3}\), \(83\ \mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{decade}^{-1}\) and \(4.7\ \mathrm{A}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC-4}\), and \(93\ \mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{decade}^{-1}\) and \(7.9\ \mathrm{A}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for pure \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\). The superior performance acquired by \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC-2}\) was ascribed to its higher specific surface area \((228.0\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) than those of other \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPCs}\) \((7.62\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC-1}\), \(147.11\ \mathrm{m}^{3}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC-3}\) and \(110.85\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC-4}\)).
Yamauchi and co-workers [78] prepared a highly graphited nitrogen-doped porous carbon electrocatalyst (GC@COF-NC, GC \(=\) graphitic carbon, \(\mathrm{NC}=\) porous carbon) which exhibited high activity in ORR testing. The formation of GC@COF-NC hybrid materials followed the below processes: Firstly, ZIF-67 was carbonized to form GC; secondly, the as-prepared GC was introduced into the synthetic process of DAAQ-TFP-COF \((\mathrm{DAAQ} = 2,6\)-diaminoanthraquinone, and \(\mathrm{TFP} = 1,3,5\)-triformylphloroglucinol) to make COF grow homogeneously on the GC surface; finally, the as-prepared GC@COF was thermally converted to GC@COF-NC materials through carbonization under inert nitrogen atmosphere. The GC@COF with different concentrations of COF monomer (0.01, 0.04, and \(0.08\ \mathrm{mM}\)) was pyrolyzed at \(800\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to obtain the GC@COF-\(\mathrm{NC}_{0.01}\), GC@COF-\(\mathrm{NC}_{0.04}\) and GC@COF-\(\mathrm{NC}_{0.08}\), respectively. According to ORR measurement results, the oxygen reduction characteristic peak intensity followed the sequence of GC@COF-\(\mathrm{NC}_{0.04}\) \((0.796\ \mathrm{V}) > \mathrm{GC@COF-NC}_{0.01}\) \((0.793\ \mathrm{V}) > \mathrm{pure}\ \mathrm{GC}\) \((0.765\ \mathrm{V}) > \mathrm{GC@COF-NC}_{0.08}\) \((0.762\ \mathrm{V})\). Moreover, the onset potential \((E_{\mathrm{onset}})\) calculated
when the current density reached \(5\%\) of the limiting current density) and half-wave potential \((E_{1/2})\) both obeyed the following sequence: GC@COF-\(\mathrm{NC}_{0.04}\) \((E_{\mathrm{onset}} = 0.923\ \mathrm{V}\) and \(E_{1/2} = 0.8441\ \mathrm{V})\) \(>\) GC@COF-\(\mathrm{NC}_{0.01}\) \((0.913\) and \(0.837\ \mathrm{V})\) \(>\) pure GC \((0.912\) and \(0.830\ \mathrm{V})\) \(>\) GC@COF-\(\mathrm{NC}_{0.08}\) \((0.874\) and \(0.811\ \mathrm{V})\), further demonstrating the superior ORR activity of GC@COF-\(\mathrm{NC}_{0.04}\).
