## 4.2 Energy storage
Recently, Wang and co-workers [143] reported COF/Mn-MOF hybrid materials and their derivate \(\mathrm{MnS@NS-C}\) composites for lithium storages. When being tested as the negative electrode for lithium-ion batteries, as-resultant COF/Mn-MOF showed a capacity of \(1,015\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) after 650 cycles, larger than that of the sole Mn-MOFs \((258\;\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) and COFs \((126\;\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) . In addition, \(\mathrm{MnS@NS-C-g}\) follow \((1,068\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) and \(\mathrm{MnS@NS-C-}\) l core–shell \((1,017\;\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) microspheres, the derivatives of COF/Mn-MOF synthesized in the gas-phase and liquid-phase, respectively, showed greatly increased lithium-storage capabilities, attributed to the greatly improved conductivity and doping of N and S active sites.
4.2 Energy storageRecently, Wang and co-workers [143] reported COF/Mn-MOF hybrid materials and their derivate \(\mathrm{MnS@NS-C}\) composites for lithium storages. When being tested as the negative electrode for lithium-ion batteries, as-resultant COF/Mn-MOF showed a capacity of \(1,015\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) after 650 cycles, larger than that of the sole Mn-MOFs \((258\;\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) and COFs \((126\;\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) . In addition, \(\mathrm{MnS@NS-C-g}\) follow \((1,068\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) and \(\mathrm{MnS@NS-C-}\) l core–shell \((1,017\;\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) microspheres, the derivatives of COF/Mn-MOF synthesized in the gas-phase and liquid-phase, respectively, showed greatly increased lithium-storage capabilities, attributed to the greatly improved conductivity and doping of N and S active sites.Supercapacitors, as a promising type of energy storage device have attracted increasing attentions. Samori and co-workers [130] reported a supercapacitor electrode fabricated based on the aza-MOF@COF hybrid material. According to electrochemical characterizations, the anal capacitance of the supercapacitors followed the sequence of aza-MOF@COF \((20.35\;\mathrm{\mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}})\;>\) \(\mathrm{MOF@COF-LZU1}\) \((9.56\;\mathrm{\mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}})\;>\;\mathrm{MOF{-}U i O{-}66{-}N H_{2}}\) (7.89 \(\mathrm{mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}})\) . Moreover, the corresponding stack capacitance of aza-MOF@COF was calculated as \(1.16\;\mathrm{F{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) , which was nearly two times higher than those of pure MOF-UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{MOF@COF-LZU1}\) materials. Importantly, the extended \(\pi\) - delocalization and decreased pore size (from ca. \(1\,\textrm{n m}\) to ca. \(0.7\;\mathrm{nm}\) ) in aza-MOF@COF were suggested to play a critical role in enhancing the performance of the supercapacitor.
## 5 Conclusion and prospects
5 Conclusion and prospectsThis review has thoroughly described the development of novel porous organic frameworks via combination of the covalent chemistry of COFs and coordination chemistry of MOFs. For examples, the imine and B–O bonds popularly employed in linking COFs have been introduced to construct unusual MOFs, which are formidable to be obtained through the conventional metal–ligand coordination. With the aid of directional coordination bond, meanwhile, novel woven frameworks have been successfully obtained in good crystallinity. Impressively, after removal of metal species, as-obtained metal-free woven frameworks have exhibited dynamic and adaptive abilities toward incoming substrates. Moreover, MOF and COF hybrids have also been extensively exploited for integration of both advantages of the individual components. Such colorful chemistry nature of aforementioned porous materials has also endowed them with multifunctionalities required for exhibiting superior performances in versatile application fields including adsorption, separation, catalysis, biosensing and energy storage. Nevertheless, there are still lots of challenges remaining to be resolved in this nascent research field and hence the corresponding perspectives are also provided herein on basis of our personal insights.(1) Dynamic covalent bonds bring new opportunity for construction of novel MOFs especially whose secondary metal nodes are incompatible with the conventional MOF synthesis conditions. However, current successful examples are merely constructed via formation of the typical imine and B–O bonds, seriously impeding the expansion of MOFs scope as well as their further multifunctionality. In fact, other covalent bonds including amide (CO–N), carbon-nitrogen (C–N), alkene \(\scriptstyle(\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C})\) , and azodioxide \((\mathrm{N}{=}\mathrm{N})\) have already been adopted for construction of various types of COFs, which are also potentially workable for MOFs explorations through judicious design and selection of functionalized metal precursors and organic ligands.(2) Similar challenges exist in the synthesis protocols of MOF and COF hybrids, in which the hybridization procedure is also
(1) Dynamic covalent bonds bring new opportunity for construction of novel MOFs especially whose secondary metal nodes are incompatible with the conventional MOF synthesis conditions. However, current successful examples are merely constructed via formation of the typical imine and B–O bonds, seriously impeding the expansion of MOFs scope as well as their further multifunctionality. In fact, other covalent bonds including amide (CO–N), carbon-nitrogen (C–N), alkene \(\scriptstyle(\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C})\) , and azodioxide \((\mathrm{N}{=}\mathrm{N})\) have already been adopted for construction of various types of COFs, which are also potentially workable for MOFs explorations through judicious design and selection of functionalized metal precursors and organic ligands.
(2) Similar challenges exist in the synthesis protocols of MOF and COF hybrids, in which the hybridization procedure is also
limited by the typical imine linkage between MOFs and COFs. Hence, other covalent bonds-based linkages are also waiting for being adopted for hybridizing MOFs and COFs. Moreover, the coordination induced interlinkage [143] proposed by Wang et. al. is very promising to be promoted for universal construction of MOF and COF hybrids, as the unsaturated coordination metal centers and electron pair donating organic groups are easily available from MOFs and COFs components, respectively. Hence, more successful novel MOF and COF hybrid examples are foreseeable in the near future.
(3) It is still formidable to well control the morphology and structure of MOF and COF hybrids due to the very little knowledge of the formation mechanism and mutual interaction. In-situ tools (e.g., operando X-ray diffraction and cryotransmission electron microscopy) are very useful for monitoring the formation kinetics and low-dose transmission electron microscopy is crucial for observing the interface of heterocomponent at molecular or even atomic scale, which may in turn provide theory principles for guiding controllable and subtle synthesis of MOF and COF hybrids.
(4) Although lots of achievements have been made for MOF and COF hybrids in application fields of adsorption, separation, catalysis, it is still in its infancy and even virgin stage like in the fields of biomaterials and optoelectronic devices. Apart from the efforts devoted to development of emerging synthesis protocols, fundamentally studying the characters of each component as well as exploration of exceptional properties stemming from the synergistic effect between inter-components is urgently desirable, not only in order to optimize their performance but also greatly expand their application scope.
Before ending, we hope the current challenges will encourage and trigger much more efforts to be paid on this emerging research field. With increasing quests from all over the world, we are confident that novel porous materials combining chemistry characts of both MOFs and COFs will have a bright prospect in the incoming future.
